// Regression test: two structs that hold each other by value, closing a
// cycle across BOTH declarations rather than within a single one, must be
// rejected the same as a direct self-cycle (TYPE-001, see
// fail_self_containing_struct.tr for the full rationale). Neither `A` nor
// `B` is individually self-referential here -- the cycle only exists once
// both declarations are considered together, which is exactly why the
// acyclicity check has to run once every struct name is hoisted (end of
// Resolver Pass 1), not inline inside each struct's own visitStructDecl().
//
// Also exercises that the cycle is found regardless of DECLARATION ORDER:
// `A` (which contains `B`) is declared before `B` (which contains `A`),
// so this only works because Pass 1 hoists every struct name before this
// check runs -- a forward reference from `A` to the not-yet-declared `B`
// must still resolve for the cycle to be seen at all.

struct A {
    b: B
}

struct B {
    a: A
}

fn main(): int {
    return 0;
}
